    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d111">

                <pb facs="0221" n="187" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg187"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/5–252</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Telephone Conversation, by Barbara Evans
                        of the Office of the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1952-05-02">May 2,
                        1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>Participants:</label>
                    <item>Mr. Short</item>
                    <item>Mr. Acheson</item>
                </list>
                <p>Mr. Short telephoned the Secretary today and referred to Syngman Rhee’s letter of
                    March 21 to President Truman<anchor n="1" xml:id="d111-f1"/> and to the
                    Secretary’s memorandum to the President<anchor n="2" xml:id="d111-f2"/>
                    recommending that there be no reply. Mr. Short said that the President thought
                    that the letter should have a reply. The President had said he thought Rhee’s
                    letter was a very good letter and deserved a reply and that the reply should be
                    sent at an early date. Mr. Short asked if the Secretary thought there was a
                    possibility of a non-committal reply.</p>
                <p>The Secretary said he thought there could be, but the troublesome thing was the
                    question Rhee raised of the possibility of a security treaty. The Secretary
                    pointed out that we could not enter into this and it would be difficult to write
                    a letter which would ignore it or brush it off. We could not give him
                    encouragement. The Secretary said that in answer to the President’s feeling that
                    it would be discourteous not to reply, that any letter sent to Rhee would be a
                    reply to the Rhee reply to the President’s previous letter, and that this might
                    involve a long correspondence which would be difficult to shut off.</p>
                <p>Mr. Short then suggested that maybe some sort of message could be gotten
                    informally through Muccio to Rhee giving him the President’s greetings and
                    saying that the President was pleased with Rhee’s letter. Mr. Acheson said he
                    thought this was a good suggestion and he would <pb facs="0222" n="188"
                        xml:id="pg188"/> try to have Mr. Allison draft something to be submitted to
                    the President when Mr. Acheson sees him on Monday.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d111-f3"
                    /></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d111-f1" n="1" xml:id="d111-f1-content">
                <p>For the text of this letter, see <ref target="#pg114">p. 114</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d111-f2" n="2" xml:id="d111-f2-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic"><ref target="#d111">Supra</ref></hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d111-f3" n="3" xml:id="d111-f3-content">
                <p>Acheson met with Truman on Monday, May 5. He showed the President a reply worked
                    out by Allison and Short and the President approved it. (Secretary’s Memoranda
                    with the President, lot 65 D 238) For the text of the reply to Rhee, see
                    memorandum by Allison, May 2, <ref target="#pg189">p. 189</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
